   "title": "Down the Diagonal",
   "level": 1,
   "statement": "<p>A square matrix is given. Its <em>main diagonal</em> runs from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner: the 1st number of row 1, the 2nd number of row 2, and so on. Print the numbers on it and their sum.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds <code>n</code> integers.</p>",
   "output": "<p>Two lines: the diagonal numbers from top to bottom, separated by single spaces, then their sum.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n ≤ 30; every number is between −1000 and 1000.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "3\n1 2 3\n4 5 6\n7 8 9\n",
     "out": "1 5 9\n15\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>The cells on the main diagonal are the ones whose row index equals their column index.</p>",
   "solution": "n = int(input())\na = [list(map(int, input().split())) for _ in range(n)]\nd = [a[i][i] for i in range(n)]\nprint(*d)\nprint(sum(d))",
