   "title": "Blurring a Picture",
   "level": 3,
   "statement": "<p>A simple way to blur a grey-scale picture: every pixel becomes the average of itself and its 8 neighbours. Pixels at the edge or in a corner have fewer neighbours, and only the pixels that exist are averaged. The average is rounded down to a whole number. All new values are worked out from the <em>original</em> picture.</p>",
   "input": "<p>The first line holds <code>n</code> and <code>m</code>. Each of the next <code>n</code> lines holds <code>m</code> pixels.</p>",
   "output": "<p>The blurred picture: <code>n</code> lines of <code>m</code> numbers separated by single spaces.</p>",
   "constraints": "<p>1 ≤ n, m ≤ 30; 0 ≤ pixel ≤ 255.</p>",
   "examples": [
    {
     "in": "2 3\n10 20 30\n40 50 60\n",
     "out": "30 35 40\n30 35 40\n"
    },
    {
     "in": "1 3\n0 1 255\n",
     "out": "0 85 128\n"
    }
   ],
   "hint": "<p>For each pixel, add up the values in the 3 × 3 block around it that lie inside the picture, count how many there were, and use <code>//</code>.</p>",
   "solution": "n, m = map(int, input().split())\na = [list(map(int, input().split())) for _ in range(n)]\nfor i in range(n):\n    out = []\n    for j in range(m):\n        total = count = 0\n        for r in range(i - 1, i + 2):\n            for c in range(j - 1, j + 2):\n                if 0 <= r < n and 0 <= c < m:\n                    total += a[r][c]\n                    count += 1\n        out.append(total // count)\n    print(*out)",
